<Doc
  title="Infrastructure stays silent"
  kicker="Track K — Keep Infrastructure Silent"
  description="Repositories as in-memory collections, plain objects, and mapping at the boundary. The database is a detail."
  path="/guide/silent-infrastructure"
>
  <p class="lede">
    The database is the most expensive detail in most systems and the least
    interesting. Silence means the domain can be reasoned about without knowing which
    engine, which ORM, or whether a transaction is open.
  </p>

  <h2>A repository is a collection</h2>

  <p>
    The word is literal. A repository behaves like an in-memory collection of
    aggregates: you get one, you add one, you save what you have. No query builders, no
    SQL fragments, no lazy-loading proxies crossing the boundary.
  </p>

  <CodeTabs
    title="Port and adapter"
    tabs={[
      { id: 'php', label: 'Port', code: phpPort },
      { id: 'cs', label: 'Adapter', code: phpAdapter },
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    The port has no <code>findByEmailLikedSince</code>. Query objects exist for
    reports; the repository serves the use cases’ need for aggregates.
  </p>

  <h2>Plain objects</h2>

  <p>
    Entities are POPO, POCO, POJO — the acronym is the only thing that changes.
    Equality is by identity, state is private, behaviour is public, and the object does
    not know it is persisted.
  </p>

  <CodeTabs
    title="The same object, two ecosystems"
    tabs={[
      { id: 'cs', label: 'C# — POCO', code: csPoco },
      { id: 'java', label: 'Java — record', code: javaValue },
      { id: 'ts', label: 'TypeScript', code: tsValue },
    ]}
  />

  <Note label="Rule of thumb" variant="rule">
    <p>
      If a domain file contains an annotation, an attribute, a base class or a
      serialization hint, infrastructure is talking. Move it to a mapping class.
    </p>
  </Note>

  <h2>Mapping belongs to the boundary</h2>

  <p>
    Two models, two jobs. The domain model protects invariants; the persistence model
    protects a schema. They usually start identical and must be allowed to diverge —
    the mapping class is where that divergence is allowed to exist.
  </p>

  <p>
    In C# with EF Core that is a <code>DbContext</code> configuration class. In Java
    with JPA, a mapper or a separate entity. In C++ with a handwritten store, a mapper
    function. In PHP with Doctrine, an XML/YAML mapping or attributes kept outside the
    domain namespace.
  </p>

  <h2>Transactions are infrastructure</h2>

  <p>
    A use case says “save these two accounts together”; whether that becomes
    <code>BEGIN/COMMIT</code>, a unit of work, or a saga is the adapter’s decision. If
    the domain calls <code>beginTransaction()</code>, the database has become part of
    the business rules.
  </p>

  <h2>The in-memory implementation</h2>

  <p>
    Write it on day one. It is the cheapest proof that the port is honest and the use
    case is free of infrastructure:
  </p>

  <CodeTabs
    title="A fake repository is a test fixture and an architecture test"
    tabs={[{ id: 'cpp', label: 'C++ port', code: cppPort }]}
  />

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Language</th><th>Typical persistence detail</th><th>Where it belongs</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>PHP</td><td>Doctrine EntityManager, PDO</td><td><code>Infrastructure/</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>C#</td><td>DbContext, Dapper, EF migrations</td><td>adapter project</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Java</td><td>JPA EntityManager, JDBC</td><td><code>infrastructure</code> module</td></tr>
        <tr><td>C++</td><td>SQL client, connection pool</td><td>adapter translation unit</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>What silence buys you</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Unit tests that run in milliseconds, with no database container.</li>
    <li>The ability to change the engine without touching the domain.</li>
    <li>Errors that mean something: <code>InsufficientFunds</code>, not <code>SQLSTATE[23000]</code>.</li>
    <li>A domain that can be read by someone who has never seen the schema.</li>
  </ul>

  <Note label="Common objection" variant="warn">
    <p>
      “Two models is duplication.” It is duplication the same way a seatbelt is
      friction. The moment the schema needs a column the domain should not know about,
      the duplication pays for itself.
    </p>
  </Note>
</Doc>
